MCON 141 — The Midnight Carnival
Programming Assignment — 100 Points

Time allowed: One week
Estimated completion time: 6–7 hours; approximately 5 hours for a fast student
Submission: One Python .py file and a brief testing record

Your Mission
Welcome to the Midnight Carnival!

The carnival opens at sunset, and you have been hired to write the computer program that keeps everything running.

Your program will welcome visitors, check admission requirements, sell game tickets, operate a prize booth, and report how the evening went.

Unfortunately, the carnival has no computer system yet. You will build it!

You will complete five challenges. Each challenge adds functionality to your program. By the end, you should have one working carnival application.

Challenge 1 — The Carnival Entrance (15 points)
Every visitor must pass through the entrance.

Ask each visitor for:

Their name.

Their age (a whole number).

Whether they have a VIP pass (yes or no).

Admission rules:

Visitors younger than 8 must be accompanied by an adult. For this project, assume they cannot enter on their own.

Visitors aged 8–17 may enter for $5.

Visitors aged 18 or older pay $10.

Visitors of any permitted age with a VIP pass enter free.

Your program must use an if/elif/else structure and compound Boolean logic where appropriate.

Print a welcoming message indicating whether the visitor may enter and the admission charge.

For this challenge, you may assume the visitor enters a valid age and a valid VIP response.

Challenge 2 — The Ticket Booth (20 points)
Visitors need tickets to play carnival games.

Each ticket costs $2.

Ask the visitor how many tickets they wish to purchase. Accept whole numbers from 1 through 20.

Your program must:

Use a while loop to keep asking until a valid number is entered.

Use try/except to handle input that cannot be converted to an integer.

Explicitly raise ValueError if the number is outside 1–20.

Display a helpful message for invalid input.

Use the else clause associated with try to accept a valid purchase and end the validation loop.

Calculate and display the ticket cost.

Example:

How many tickets? abc
Please enter a whole number.

How many tickets? 25
Tickets must be between 1 and 20.

How many tickets? 6
Accepted: 6
Ticket cost: $12
The ticket cost must be added to the visitor's total charges.

Challenge 3 — The Lucky Number Game (20 points)
Visitors may use their purchased tickets to play the Lucky Number Game.

Each play costs one ticket.

For each play, ask the visitor to guess a whole number between 1 and 5.

The carnival's winning number is 3.

Scoring rules:

Correct guess: award 10 prize points.

Incorrect guess: award 2 prize points.

A guess outside 1–5 earns no points, but still uses one ticket.

Use a for loop to process the number of purchased tickets, and use an accumulator to keep track of the visitor's prize points.

Use conditional statements to determine the points awarded for each guess.

Assume the visitor enters integers for the guesses.

At the end, display the total prize points earned.

Challenge 4 — The Mystery Prize Board (20 points)
The carnival has a mysterious illuminated prize board.

Your program must generate this pattern:

* - - - *
- * - * -
- - * - -
- * - * -
* - - - *
Use two nested while loops to display the five rows and five columns.

The outer loop controls the rows. The inner loop controls the columns.

Use conditional logic to decide whether to display * or -.

You must generate the pattern using loops. You may not simply print five complete rows as separate strings.

After displaying the board, print the total number of positions examined by the inner loop.

Challenge 5 — Closing Time! (15 points)
Your carnival must be able to serve multiple visitors.

Use a sentinel-controlled loop to repeat the visitor experience.

At the beginning of each visit, ask for the visitor's name. If the operator enters done, the carnival closes.

For each visitor who is admitted, run the ticket booth and Lucky Number Game, then display the prize board.

Maintain accumulators for:

Number of visitors admitted.

Total admission revenue.

Total ticket revenue.

Total prize points awarded.

Visitors who are denied admission must not purchase tickets or play games.

When the operator enters done, display an end-of-night report containing all four totals and the combined revenue.

Use meaningful variable names and initialize your accumulators correctly.

Final Requirements — Code Quality and Testing (10 points)
Your completed program must:

Run without syntax errors.

Use clear, consistent indentation.

Include comments explaining important decisions, loops, and accumulators.

Display understandable prompts and results.

Use only the Python concepts covered in class through Exam 1.

You are not required to use functions, arguments, return statements, dictionaries, classes, files, or imported modules.

Testing record: At the bottom of your Python file, include a comment section describing at least five tests you performed, the inputs used, and whether the results were correct.

Your tests must include a denied visitor, a VIP visitor, an invalid ticket quantity, a nonnumeric ticket entry, and multiple visitors followed by done.

